---
title: Smach Burger
source: direct-input
servings: 1
yield: 1burger
---

Divide the @ground beef{1%lb} into @?balls{3-4%oz}, depending on your preference. A 3 oz patty will cook up thinner and crispier, while a 4 oz patty will be slightly thicker. Roll them gently into loose balls without overworking the meat.

Spread a thin layer of @mayonnaise{} on the cut sides of the @brioche buns{4}. Toast them in a #skillet{} or on a #griddle{} until golden brown and crisp. Set aside.

Heat a #cast iron skillet{} or #griddle{} over medium-high heat until it’s very hot. Place the beef balls onto the hot surface and let them sear for ~{10-15%seconds}. Cover each ball with parchment paper and use a burger press or heavy spatula to firmly smash them flat. Season with @kosher salt{2%pinches} and @freshly ground black pepper{1%tsp} or the homemade seasoning blend. Season with @garlic powder{2%tsp}, @onion powder{2%tsp}, @paprika{1%tsp}, and @dried oregano{1/2%tsp} as well. Cook until the edges are browned and juices rise to the surface.

Flip the patties with a sturdy spatula, breaking the crust free from the pan. Immediately place a slice of @American cheese{4} on top of each patty after flipping. Allow the cheese to melt while the second side finishes cooking.

Spread burger sauce on the toasted buns. Place one patty—or stack two if making doubles—on the bottom bun. Add @pickles{} and @?bacon{1}(cooked crisp) if using. Finish with the top bun and more sauce if desired.
